---
/**
 * Where the data came from, what was done to it, and what this site does not
 * claim.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { LAB, PMEL, REPO } from '../config';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout title="About">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="topline">
      <h1 class="figure-title">About</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        Tracks, time series and quality control for every Saildrone, Oshen and
        Chance Maritime uncrewed surface vehicle published on
        the <a href={PMEL}>NOAA PMEL ERDDAP</a> — 153 deployments, 2017 to now,
        active and historic.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
      <p>
        Everything here is NOAA's. The vehicles are operated by PMEL, AOML and
        NEFSC with Saildrone Inc., Oshen and Chance Maritime Technologies, and
        published on the PMEL ERDDAP. Each record's page links to its own
        ERDDAP dataset, which carries the licence and the acknowledgement that
        govern it; this site republishes neither more nor differently.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>This site is not live, and it cannot be.</strong>
        <code>data.pmel.noaa.gov</code> sends
        no <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> header on any response, so a
        page in your browser cannot read it at all. The data here was fetched
        by the build, checked, and written out as JSON this origin serves.
        Every page prints when its record was fetched. If you need the current
        hour, follow the ERDDAP link on the record and take it from the source.
      </p>

      <h2>What was done to the numbers</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Nothing was removed and nothing was smoothed.</strong> Three
        things were changed, and all three are stated on the page where they
        apply:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Units were converted</strong> where the archive is
          inconsistent. Every Oshen publishes wind in knots and every other
          vehicle in m/s; one Chance column publishes a bearing in radians.
          Each record's quality report lists every conversion applied to it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Records were decimated</strong> to at most 8,000 points, by
          keeping every <em>n</em>th real sample rather than by averaging — an
          average would put numbers on screen that no instrument reported. The
          quality checks ran <em>before</em> that, on the finest resolution the
          build fetched.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Values were rounded</strong> to what each instrument
          resolves: sea temperature to 0.0001 °C, pressure to 0.01 hPa,
          position to five decimals, and so on.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What was computed here</h2>
      <p>
        Seven quantities are derived rather than published, and each is marked
        with an <span class="mono">ƒ</span> in the variable list:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>U₁₀</strong>, the wind adjusted to 10 m through a neutral
          logarithmic profile from the sensor's own height — 0.66 m on an
          Oshen, about 3.4 m on a Saildrone and published per record because
          the wing moves. <strong>Without it the two cannot be compared:</strong>
          the Oshen adjustment is +31 %. Neutral is an assumption, not a
          measurement; in a strongly stable surface layer it over-corrects.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Wind stress</strong>, ρ<sub>a</sub> C<sub>D</sub> U₁₀², with
          the Large &amp; Pond (1981) drag coefficient.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Dewpoint</strong> and <strong>specific humidity</strong>,
          from the Magnus form with Alduchov &amp; Eskridge (1996)
          coefficients.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Absolute Salinity, Conservative Temperature, σ₀, spiciness
          and sound speed</strong>, from TEOS-10 at the surface. Every variable
          named <em>pressure</em> on a USV is atmospheric, so the sea pressure
          is a constant 0 dbar — feeding a barometer to a seawater routine as
          decibars inflates in-situ density by 4.3 kg/m³ and sound speed by
          17 m/s, and every number that comes back still looks like seawater.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the quality checks do and do not claim</h2>
      <p>
        The archive publishes no quality flags on almost all of this data, so
        every quality statement here was made by this site.
        The <a href={withBase('/qc/')}>quality page</a> says what each of the nine
        checks looks for and — more usefully — what each one is <em>not</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A finding is a second opinion, not a correction. It marks the data and
        never alters it. A record with nothing found is a record these nine
        checks found nothing in, which is a smaller claim than "clean".
      </p>

      <h2>What is not here</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Analysis.</strong> Intercomparison, storm context and glider
          collocation live
          in <a href={LAB.analysis}>NOAA-USV-analysis</a>. This site shows what
          the vehicles measured and what is wrong with it; it does not draw
          conclusions from it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vertical structure.</strong> These are surface vehicles.
          Eleven Chance Maritime file listings hold CTD casts, echosounder
          files and imagery behind per-cast files — real profile data, and the
          only vertical structure this archive has. Reading them is open work.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Every column.</strong> Each record carries at most 40 of its
          variables here, chosen canonical-first. The ERDDAP link on every page
          has all of them at full resolution.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Credits</h2>
      <p>
        Built by <strong>Donglai Gong</strong> (Virginia Institute of Marine
        Science, William &amp; Mary) at
        the <a href={LAB.url}>Collaboratory for Physical Oceanography</a>, with
        Claude (Anthropic). Source at <a href={REPO}>oceansensing/usv</a>; all
        rights reserved.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sibling sites: <a href={LAB.gliders}>gliders</a>, for the IOOS Glider
        DAC, which shares this one's plot engine and TEOS-10 implementation.
        Basemap by Esri — GEBCO, NOAA, National Geographic.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    margin-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-xs);
  }

  li { margin-block-end: var(--space-xs); }
</style>
